<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesoamerica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mesoamerican</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesoamerica is the cultural region of central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> where civilisations such as the Olmec, Maya, Teotihuacan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> developed. They shared features such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> agriculture, calendars, writing and pyramid temples. The Aztec Empire was the dominant Mesoamerican power at the time of the Spanish conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesoamerica.jpg" alt="Mesoamerica" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yavidaxiu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mesoamerica_english.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The tributary system, paradoxically, preserved more of <b>Mesoamerica</b>'s political and cultural diversity than the Aztecs' reputation as conquerors might suggest: by governing through local rulers rather than replacing them, the empire left in place administrative structures and traditions that the Spanish colonial system could later co-opt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its art joined Maya traditions to the international style of Postclassic <b>Mesoamerica</b>: its murals and sculpture share themes with the Mixteca-Puebla codices of southern Mexico, and temple groups and effigy censers in its style appear from the east coast of Yucatán to the Petén lakes and highland Guatemala.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Besides the Purépecha, whose language is unrelated to any other in <b>Mesoamerica</b>, the kingdom included Nahua, Otomí, Matlatzinca and other peoples, who kept their own languages but owed tribute and, in war, warriors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Workshops turned obsidian from the nearby Otumba sources and the green obsidian of the Sierra de las Navajas near Pachuca into blades and points, and green Teotihuacan obsidian travelled widely across <b>Mesoamerica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Tlaxcalan troops went on to fight in Spanish campaigns across <b>Mesoamerica</b>, including the conquest of Guatemala led by Pedro de Alvarado in the 1520s and the Mixtón War of 1540–1542.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Tula’s art and architecture have close parallels elsewhere in <b>Mesoamerica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
